  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Facts → holding', note: 'A court decides a case and, in doing so, states a rule that justified the outcome — the ratio decidendi ("the reason for deciding").' },
    { math: '2 · The rule outlives the case', note: 'Stare decisis ("to stand by things decided"): later courts must apply that rule to cases with materially similar facts.' },
    { math: '3 · Distinguishing', note: 'A later court can escape a precedent by showing the facts genuinely differ — the rule does not reach this case. Much legal writing is exactly this move.' },
    { math: '4 · Overruling', note: 'Only a higher court (or the same one, sitting again) may overrule. Precedent is sticky by design: law values predictability.' },
