<TopicPage slug="subjects/law/foundational/what-makes-a-law">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    "Law" is not one thing but a stack of <strong>sources</strong>, each trumping the one below:
    a <strong>constitution</strong> sets the frame; <strong>statutes</strong> are what
    legislatures write; <strong>regulations</strong> are the executive filling in detail;
    <strong>precedent</strong> is what courts said it all means; and custom quietly fills the
    gaps. A lawyer's first skill is knowing which source controls a question — and what to do
    when two seem to conflict.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="precedent — how one case quietly becomes the law for the next thousand" widget="equationStep" config={precedentCfg} />

  <h2>Two great divides</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Criminal vs civil.</strong> Criminal law punishes offences against society — prison, fines — and requires guilt "beyond reasonable doubt". Civil law settles disputes between parties — damages, orders — on the "balance of probabilities". O. J. Simpson was acquitted criminally but liable civilly: same events, different standards of proof.</li>
    <li><strong>Common law vs civil law systems.</strong> Common-law countries (UK, US, India) build law through judicial precedent; civil-law countries (most of Europe, Latin America) start from comprehensive codes. Most modern systems mix both.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What makes an answer "legal reasoning"</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>It cites authority</strong> — a statute, a case, a constitutional clause — not just opinion.</li>
    <li><strong>It applies rules to facts</strong> and says exactly which facts matter and why.</li>
    <li><strong>It confronts the counter-argument</strong> — the strongest reading against you — before answering it.</li>
    <li><strong>It asks who decides.</strong> Half of lawyering is jurisdiction: which court, which law applies, who has standing to ask.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Law is also a moral technology under permanent repair: every rule you will read in this
    subject's ladder exists because someone, somewhere, was harmed in a way the previous rules
    couldn't answer.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> standard first-year treatments (contracts, torts, constitutional law); Glanville Williams, Learning the Law.</p>
</TopicPage>
